oups”, arXiv:2311.04489 (2023).\n\n# Conjecture on realizing minimal-base cardinalities for transitive permutation groups\n\nAbout 3 years old · traced to 2023\n\nLet $X\\subseteq\\mathbb{N}$X⊆N with $1\\notin X$1∈/X. For a permutation group $G$G acting transitively on a domain $\\Omega$Ω, let $\\mathcal{M}(G,\\Omega)$M(G,Ω) denote the set of cardinalities of minimal bases of $G$G on $\\Omega$Ω. Realization conjecture. There exists a transitive permutation group $G$G on $\\Omega$Ω such that\n\n$$\\mathcal{M}(G,\\Omega)=X.$$M(G,Ω)=X.\n\nThe conjecture proposes that every subset of the natural numbers not containing $1$1 can occur as the set of minimal-base cardinalities for a transitive permutation group. The source gives examples for certain non-interval subsets but notes that, for example, $\\{4,6\\}${4,6} is not known to be realizable.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nFrancesca Dalla Volta, Fabio Mastrogiacomo and Pablo Spiga, “On the cardinality of irredundant and minimal bases of finite permutation groups”, arXiv:2311.04489 (2023).\n\nbasespermutation-groups\n\n0 0\n\nPosted 1 month ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nLOOMS\nRON AHARONI, ELI BERGER, JOSEPH BRIGGS, HE G (https://heguo.site/files/papers/looms.pdf)\nciteturn10search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10 months ago; LOOMS ... By Theorem 2.6, if r = s then this would imply that max(τ ∗(A), τ ∗(B)) = r. ... side is a cover this implies τ(L) ⩽r. ... original conjecture (bounding the covering number by 2r −2), because we used the ... A hypergraph H is said to be pinnable if H⊥̸= ∅.\nP\ne∈A:v∈e w(e) = 1 for every v ∈V . Thus we have\nX\nX\n|V (A)| =\nX