ournal of Algebraic Combinatorics (2024) 60:569–587                                                                 571\n\nThe proof of Theorem 1.1 is due to Peter Cameron. It should be noted that this proof remains unpublished, except for its appearance on Cameron’s blog.¹ For the sake of completeness, we include the proof of Theorem 1.1 in Sect. 2.\n\nIn the light of this, Theorem 1.2 comes as a surprise, because it shows that any set of positive integers arises as 𝓜(G, Ω) for some permutation group G. In particular, 𝓜(G, Ω) is not an interval of natural numbers.\n\nThe next question to explore is the behaviour of 𝓜(G, Ω) when G is a transitive group. Theorem 1.3 shows that every interval of positive integers, not containing the number 1, can be realized as 𝓜(G, Ω) for some transitive permutation group G acting on its domain Ω. It is important to note that if 1 ∈ 𝓜(G, Ω) and G is transitive, then 𝓜(G, Ω) = {1}, because G is regular on Ω.\n\nWhile this last result might suggest that 𝓜(G, Ω) is always an interval when G is transitive, we prove in Sect. 4 that this is not universally true. In fact, we show that for certain non-interval subsets X ⊆ ℕ, there exists a transitive permutation group G acting on its domain Ω such that 𝓜(G, Ω) = X. However, we encounter limitations when addressing the problem in a general context. For instance, we are unable to find an example with 𝓜(G, Ω) = {4, 6}. Nevertheless, we propose the following conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 1.4** Let X ⊆ ℕ, with 1 ∉ X. Then, there exists a transitive permutation group G on Ω with 𝓜(G, Ω) = X.\n\nWe conclude this introductory section by observing that we know very little about the structure of 𝓜(G, Ω) when G is primitive on Ω. Indeed, in the case of primitive groups, we have no example where 𝓜(G, Ω) is not an interval. This leads us to propose the following conjecture.\n\n**Conjecture 1.5** Let G be a primitive permutation group on Ω. Then, 𝓜(G, Ω) is an interval of natural numbers.\n\nWe do not believe that, for each interval X of natural numbers, there exists a primitive group G on Ω with 𝓜(G, Ω) = X. We give some evidence of this in Sect. 5.\n\nA permutation group G on Ω is said to be IBIS (**Irrredundant Bases of Invariant Size**) if all irredundant bases have cardinality b(G, Ω), that is, 𝓘(G, Ω) = {b(G, Ω)}. Cameron and Fon-der-Flaass [2] (see also [1, Section 4.14]) have proved that, in a finite permutation group, the following conditions are equivalent:\n• all irredundant bases have the same size;\n• the irredundant bases are invariant under re-ordering;\n• the irredundant bases are the bases of a matroid.\n\nObserve that, for every permutation group G on Ω, we have 𝓜(G, Ω) ⊆ 𝓘(G, Ω). Therefore, we say that G is MiBIS (**Minimal Bases of Invariant Size**) if all minimal bases have cardinality b(G, Ω), that is, 𝓜(G, Ω) = {b(G, Ω)}. It is evident that each IBIS group is also a MiBIS group, but the reverse is far from being true.² This leads us\n\n¹ https://cameroncoun...wordpress.com/2023/04/15/bases-2/.\n\n² The action of G = GL₄(2) on the 2-subspaces of F₂⁴ has irredundant bases of size 4 and 5; however, it can be verified with the auxiliary help of a computer that every minimal base of G has cardinality 4. Therefore, in this action, G is MiBIS, but not IBIS.\n\nSpringer--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCombinatorics: AI research tracker · AI Proof Watch (https://aiproofwatch.com/problems/combinatorics)\nciteturn10search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; ### The cardinality of a set containing the pairwise sums of a fixed number of integers ... arXiv:2605.00040v1 Paper date: 28 Apr 2026 ... For regular adjacent-transposition chains on $S_{n}$S n​ with $n\\ge 3$n≥3, the second-largest eigenvalue has algebraic multiplicity M equal to the number N of neutral labels, except when $N=n-2$N=n−2 or n, when $M=n-1$M=n−1.\n### The cardinality of a set containing the pairwise sums of a fixed number of integers\n\